---
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
import Ornament from "../components/Ornament.astro";
import Transmission from "../islands/Transmission.svelte";
import { withBase } from "../lib/site.js";
import { voices, transmission } from "../lib/portal-data.js";

const t = transmission();
const vs = voices().map((v) => ({ id: v.id, name: v.name, short: v.short, branch: v.branch, floruit: v.floruit }));
---

<Layout
  title="Transmission"
  description="Who took what from whom, and what they changed — the descent of the legend, motif by motif."
  current="/library"
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <span class="rubric">Transmission</span>
    <h1>One inheritance, many hands</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      This is the diagram the whole portal is arguing for. The lines are the routes along which the
      material actually travelled, and the point of the exercise is what happens to an object as it
      passes down one of them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pick a motif and watch it change. The sword starts as a Welsh war-leader&rsquo;s weapon, is
      latinised and forged in Avalon, is demoted below the Grail, and ends up split in two &mdash; a
      stone that elects a king, and a lake that lends him a blade and takes it back. Nobody
      corrupted anything. Each of them made it mean something they needed it to mean.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Transmission voices={vs} edges={t.edges} motifs={t.motifs} base={withBase("/")} client:visible />

  <Ornament />

  <section class="narrow narrow--flush">
    <span class="rubric">Two lines worth noticing</span>
    <h2>The dotted ones</h2>
    <p>
      One edge is drawn as unresolved: the Welsh tales and Chrétien tell two of the same stories,
      and a century of scholarship has not settled who borrowed from whom. Treating the Welsh
      material as straightforwardly the original is a reflex the evidence does not support, and so
      is the reverse.
    </p>
    <p>
      One edge is drawn as an absence. <em>Sir Gawain and the Green Knight</em> is the finest poem in
      the tradition and it left no mark on Malory whatsoever &mdash; it survives in a single
      manuscript and was effectively unread for four centuries. Influence is not a function of
      quality, and a diagram that only drew the successful transmissions would imply that it is.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <a href={withBase("/library/")}>The voices in order &rarr;</a> &middot;
      <a href={withBase("/timeline/")}>The same material as dates &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
